<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mattium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mattium was the chief settlement of the Chatti, a Germanic people of what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> burned it in 15 CE during his campaigns across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> never brought the Chatti under lasting control. Its site is uncertain, though it is often sought near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a> or Gudensberg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mattium.jpg" alt="Mattium" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmhkmh · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mattium2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Its exact location is unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> places Mattium north of the river Adrana, the Eder, and following his description it is generally assumed to lie somewhere in the wider neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Some linguistic analysis suggests that the names of the villages Maden and Metze and of the brook Matzoff may derive from Mattium, which would place it near them, but no archaeological find has yet given an exact location or confirmed either Tacitus or the linguistic argument.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, an ancient fortified site in the same neighbourhood, was often equated with Mattium in older literature. More recent research, in particular the dating of artefacts from the site, has concluded that it was abandoned decades before the Roman troops destroyed Mattium, and it is no longer considered a suitable candidate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> mentions Mattium in Book I, chapter 56 of his Annals, describing the Roman campaign against the Chatti under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> in 15 CE: the Roman army destroyed the caput gentis Mattium directly after crossing the Eder. The term caput gentis may denote a capital in the sense of a single large fortified settlement, but it could also refer to a central place of worship. In that case Mattium may have been a larger area of ritual sites and the settlements connected to them rather than one central settlement.</p><p>Mattium is not to be confused with Mattiacum, the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiesbaden">Wiesbaden</a>, also called Aquae Mattiacorum, the principal settlement of the Mattiaci. They were a separate tribe living between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> range, and unlike the Chatti they were incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes Germanicus</a> was built. Because Mattiaci could be understood as people from Mattium, some scholars believe they were related to the Chatti and had split off from them at an earlier time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mattium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Germanicus burned their chief centre, <b>Mattium</b>, in 15 CE, but Rome never brought them under lasting control.</blockquote><a href="/state/chatti">Chatti</a></li></ul></section>
